===========================================
MongoDB Local Development (Replica Set)
===========================================

# 1. Start MongoDB container with replica set
cd c:\Users\CC1925\Desktop\react\oauth\tindahan
docker-compose -f docker-compose.yml up -d

# 2. Initialize replica set (first time only)
docker exec tindahan-mongo mongosh --eval "rs.initiate({_id: 'rs0', members: [{_id: 0, host: 'localhost:27017'}]});"

# 3. Create admin user (first time only)
docker exec tindahan-mongo mongosh --eval "db.createUser({user: 'admin', pwd: 'password', roles: [{role: 'root', db: 'admin'}]})" admin

# 4. Check status
docker ps --filter "name=tindahan-mongo"
docker logs -f tindahan-mongo

# 5. Stop MongoDB
docker-compose -f docker-compose.yml down


===========================================
Backend + MongoDB Stack Deploy (Production)
===========================================

# 1. Go to backend folder
cd c:\Users\CC1925\Desktop\react\oauth\tindahan\backend

# 2. Build the image (REQUIRED before deploy)
docker build -t tindahan-api:latest .

# 3. Deploy stack
docker stack deploy -c docker-compose.yml tindahan

# 4. Check status
docker service ls

# 5. View logs
docker service logs tindahan_tindahan-api
docker service logs tindahan_mongodb

# Stop everything
docker stack rm tindahan


===========================================
MongoDB Keyfile Security
===========================================

WHAT IS IT:
- The mongo-keyfile is used for internal authentication between replica set members
- Required for replica set replication to work securely

PRODUCTION SECURITY OPTIONS:

1. Use Docker Secrets (Recommended for Swarm)
   - Store keyfile as a Docker secret instead of volume mount
   - Keyfile is only accessible to the container, not on host filesystem
   - Example in docker-compose.yml:
     secrets:
       - mongo-keyfile
     
   - Generate and add secret:
     docker secret create mongo-keyfile /path/to/mongo-keyfile

2. Use Environment Variable + Entrypoint
   - Store keyfile content in env var, write to temp file on startup
   - Never persists on disk

3. File Permissions (Minimum)
   - chmod 400 on the keyfile (read-only for owner)
   - Owned by root or mongodb user
   - Never commit to git (add to .gitignore)

4. Generate Strong Random Key
   - Use: openssl rand -base64 756 > mongo-keyfile
   - Minimum 6-1024 base64 characters

IMPORTANT: 
- Never commit mongo-keyfile to version control
- Restrict server access to the keyfile location
- Rotate keys periodically in production